      "body_sha256": "ac175036885c7404e2ca56c973d837b748f8b6818e6f34b3edaa2f0f464f91d5",
      "title_en": "Displaying beadwork on small felt boards: a complete guide",
      "body_en": "Most felt-board bead displays I had seen online filled one large board. But we tend to make lots of different series, and putting everything together can look cluttered.\n\nWhy small felt boards work well\nWhile browsing shopping apps, I found small felt boards in lots of colour palettes, including bright colours and Bauhaus-inspired sets. They are inexpensive—much cheaper than one large board. I had also seen large boards fall off walls. Smaller boards carry less weight, so I felt more comfortable using them and promptly ordered some!\n\nHere are a few tips from my own setup.\n\nBoard size and quantity\nMine are 30 × 30 cm, spaced 20 cm apart. You can arrange them in groups of 6, 9 or 12. Measure your own display wall and buy what fits.\n\nPutting boards up without damaging the wall\nFelt boards usually come with adhesive hook-and-loop backing, but I had read that sticking it directly to the wall can damage the surface. I looped masking tape so both sides were sticky, with one side against the wall and the other against the board. I put it along all four edges. It has been very secure in my setup.\n\nAttaching bead pieces so they stay on\nUse the hook side of hook-and-loop tape. First, firmly brush the felt with a steel scrubber or a stiff brush until the surface is fuzzy. Attach the hook tape, position the piece, then rub it up, down and sideways against the felt while pressing firmly. After a little while it holds well. This is the most secure method I have tried.\n\nI currently give each series its own board: two for Line Puppy, one for Kitty, one for Chinese-style designs, one for Lurk, and one for everything else. Now my mission is to fill them! Displaying the pieces properly makes me want to create even more, haha.",
      "date": "2026-07-15"
    },
    {
      "id": "6a56e2fc000000000c003001",
      "title": "🇨🇦🇺🇸北美插座拼豆框尺寸分享|严丝合缝",
      "body": "本人和朋友亲自实验得出最佳尺寸，已上墙且确定尺寸合适，在此分享给其他在北美想做拼豆开关框的友友们。\n\t\n图1-2为正方形双开关（尺寸为12×12cm），图3-4为长方形单开关（尺寸为12.5×8cm），大家量量自己家的，一般都差不多。\n\t\n[星R]如何根据已有图纸做出适合北美开关的拼豆图\n- 首先是确定开关框中间空出的格子\n以下是特殊烫的（澡巾会把拼豆压扁一点）\n（如果是普通烫需➖1格）\n正方形双开关需空出49×49格\n长方形单开关需空出51×33格\n图1的有些空隙，拼的时候留了50格，所以还是留49格合适。\n- 其次是根据图纸改动；先正常拼上方图案，拼到框时，先确定第一条边框线，往两边延伸，然后确定好空的四个点，把框补齐，具体看图5-7。\n- 还有一个小tips，如图8，填色时可以空一格，特殊烫没区别，拼的速度也快，只要把边包好就行。\n- 最好用带格子的拼豆板，好定位，方便很多。\n\t\n最后感谢老师图纸@芙蝶结🎀\n\t\n#拼豆实用主义[话题]# #小红书拼豆地图[话题]# #加拿大[话题]# #北美[话题]# #拼豆[话题]# #拼豆开关框[话题]# #拼豆店[话题]# #拼豆创意美学[话题]# #装饰[话题]# #公寓大改造[话题]#",
